{"name":"Silver Tree","hex":"#67be90","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"silver tree","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Leucadendron argenteum, a tree with striking silvery leaves, endemic to a small area of the Cape Peninsula in South Africa."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silver_tree"}],"entries":[{"title":"Silver Tree","blocks":[{"text":"Silver Tree is an American television and film writer, producer and director, from Petaluma, California."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Tree"},{"title":"Leucadendron argenteum","blocks":[{"text":"Leucadendron argenteum is an endangered plant species in the family Proteaceae, which is endemic to a small area of the Cape Peninsula, South Africa. Most grow in and around the city of Cape Town, but outlying populations exist near Somerset West (Silwerboomkloof), Paarl and Stellenbosch. It is a protected tree in South Africa."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Groote_Schuur_Est_-_Young_Silvertrees_-_Table_Mountain.jpg/330px-Groote_Schuur_Est_-_Young_Silvertrees_-_Table_Mountain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leucadendron_argenteum"},{"title":"Tree","blocks":[{"text":"In botany, a tree is a perennial plant with an elongated stem, or trunk, usually supporting branches and leaves. In some usages, the definition of a tree may be narrower, e.g., including only woody plants with secondary growth, only plants that are usable for timber, or only plants above a specified height. Wider definitions include taller palms, tree ferns, bananas, and bamboos."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Usamljeni_jasen_-_panoramio_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Usamljeni_jasen_-_panoramio_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tree"},{"title":"Silver","blocks":[{"text":"Silver is a chemical element; it has symbol Ag and atomic number 47. A soft, white, lustrous transition metal, it exhibits the highest electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity and reflectivity of any metal. Silver is found in the Earth's crust in the pure, free elemental form, as an alloy with gold (electrum) and other metals, and in minerals such as acanthite, argentite and chlorargyrite. Most silver is recovered as a byproduct of lead–zinc, copper and gold mining and refining."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/330px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/2_Large_Silvertree_-_Leucadendron_argenteum_-_CT.jpg/500px-2_Large_Silvertree_-_Leucadendron_argenteum_-_CT.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/2_Large_Silvertree_-_Leucadendron_argenteum_-_CT.jpg/500px-2_Large_Silvertree_-_Leucadendron_argenteum_-_CT.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Adult specimen growing on the eastern slopes of Table Mountain, near Rhodes Memorial.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2_Large_Silvertree_-_Leucadendron_argenteum_-_CT.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Leucadendron_argenteum_-_Silvertree_Forest_-_Table_Mountain_4.JPG/120px-Leucadendron_argenteum_-_Silvertree_Forest_-_Table_Mountain_4.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Leucadendron_argenteum_-_Silvertree_Forest_-_Table_Mountain_4.JPG/250px-Leucadendron_argenteum_-_Silvertree_Forest_-_Table_Mountain_4.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia